2026-05-21 14:10:44,545 - INFO - Response: Viruses are tiny infectious agents that can only replicate inside living cells. They are composed of genetic material (DNA or RNA) enclosed in a protein coat called a capsid. The capsid is surrounded by a protective envelope made of lipids and proteins. Viruses can be classified into two main groups: enveloped viruses, which have a lipid envelope surrounding the capsid, and non-enveloped viruses, which do not have an envelope.
Enveloped viruses are typically more infectious than non-enveloped viruses because they can better evade the host's immune system. Non-enveloped viruses, on the other hand, are less infectious but can still replicate inside the host cell.
Viruses can cause a wide range of diseases in humans, animals, and plants. Some common examples include influenza, HIV/AIDS, hepatitis, and the coronavirus that causes COVID-19.
